Third-party components:

  - The container images bundle third-party binaries under the GNU
    General Public License, Version 2 and Version 3, and several
    permissive licenses. The directly integrated GPL v2 components
    are:
      * Partclone       — https://partclone.org/
      * EZIO            — https://github.com/tjjh89017/ezio

    These remain licensed under GPL v2 only. They are separate
    programs: ThinForge interacts with them exclusively through
    process boundaries (subprocess invocation, gRPC, PXE-booted
    independent operating systems) and does not link against them,
    so each program stays under its own license.

    Additionally, container images install GPL v2-, GPL v3-, LGPL-,
    and permissively-licensed packages from the Alpine Linux
    repositories (e.g. qemu, btrfs-progs, dnsmasq, grub, parted,
    coreutils, bash, wget, ntfs-3g, udpcast, libtorrent-rasterbar).
    See THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md for the authoritative list.

    ThinForge orchestrates but does not distribute:
      * Clonezilla Live — https://clonezilla.org/
        (downloaded on demand at the deployment site from upstream)
      * DRBL            — https://drbl.org/
        (referenced in source repository only; not shipped)
      * NetBird         — https://netbird.io/
        (upstream container image `netbirdio/netbird`, pulled at the
        deployment site; neither repackaged nor modified)

    ThinForge runs NetBird's client daemon (`netbird service run`) as a
    separate program in its own container and addresses it only across
    process boundaries — CLI invocation and the management instance's
    HTTP API. It does not link against it. The client is licensed
    BSD-3-Clause; NetBird's server variant — management, signal, relay
    and dashboard — is AGPL-3.0-or-later and is not shipped by
    ThinForge: it runs on a separately operated instance, which
    ThinForge addresses only through its HTTP API. AGPLv3 §13
    binds the operator of such an instance only if the operator
    modifies NetBird. Where the ThinForge vendor operates it (hosted
    ThinVPN service), it runs from the official upstream container
    images, unmodified, and the source is named voluntarily in
    WRITTEN_OFFER.md.

